## Why This Is Asked
This question tests designing a scalable, resilient streaming pipeline on GCP with real-time per-tenant isolation, schema evolution handling, and robust rollback and test strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Dataflow streaming with schema evolution handling
- Pub/Sub DLQ and idempotent writes
- BigQuery per-tenant partitions and retention policies
- IAM least privilege, cross-project boundaries
- Observability with Cloud Logging/Monitoring
- Rollback, backfill, and validation testing

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateEvent(evt) {
  // basic schema checks and type guarantees
  if (!evt.tenant_id || !evt.event_type) {
    throw new Error('Missing required fields');
  }
  return {
    ...evt,
    processed_at: new Date().toISOString(),
    schema_version: evt.version || 'v1'
  };
}
```

## Architecture Overview
- **Ingestion**: Pub/Sub topics with tenant-level filtering
- **Processing**: Dataflow with autoscaling and preemptible workers
- **Storage**: BigQuery with per-tenant partitioning and time-based retention
- **Failure Handling**: Dead-letter queues with automated retry logic
- **Monitoring**: Real-time alerts and comprehensive logging

## Why This Is Asked
Rationale: assesses ability to design an end-to-end event-driven pipeline with multi-tenant isolation, idempotence, and basic observability using familiar GCP services.

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven design with Pub/Sub and Cloud Run
- Multi-tenant isolation and per-tenant storage
- Idempotent processing via event_id
- Per-tenant BigQuery partitions; environment separation
- Observability and simple tests

## Code Example
```javascript
function isDuplicate(event, cache) {
  const key = `${event.tenant_id}:${event.event_id}`;
  if (cache.has(key)) return true;
  cache.set(key, true);
  return false;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema evolution per tenant while keeping backward compatibility?
- What are the tradeoffs of using Redis vs in-process cache for idempotence?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical ability to design basic multi-tenant isolation using GCP primitives, plus auth, observability, and testing without overengineering.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation via per-tenant resources
- JWT-derived tenant_id routing
- Single Cloud Run boundary with tenant-aware logic
- Per-tenant data storage (buckets/datasets) and strict IAM
- Observability (Cloud Logging/Monitoring) and simple test plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: extract tenant_id from JWT
function getTenantIdFromJwt(header){
  const token = header?.split(" ")[1];
  const payload = verifyAndDecode(token);
  return payload?.tenant_id;
}
```

